{{also|Fragen}}
==German==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh|vrāgen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|frāgēn}}, {{m|goh|frahen|frahēn/frāhēn}}, which is indirectly derived from {{der|de|gem-pro|*frēgōną}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*preḱ-||to ask}}.<ref>{{R:EWddS|ed=22|hw=Frage, fragen}}</ref> Cognate with {{cog|nl|vragen||to ask}}. Related to {{inh|en|ang|freġnan}}, {{m|ang|friġnan||to ask, inquire, learn}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|de|De-fragen.ogg|audio}}
* {{IPA|de|/ˈfʁaːɡən/|[ˈfʁaːɡŋ̩]}}
* {{rhymes|de|aːɡn̩}}
* {{hyphenation|de|fra|gen}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=weak|class 2=6|fragt|pres 2=frägt|fragte|past 2=frug|gefragt|past subjunctive=fragte|past subjunctive 2=früge}}

# to [[ask]] ({{m|de|nach}} for/after)
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to [[wonder]] (literally, to ask (oneself))
#: {{ux|de|Ich '''frage''' mich, wer mir helfen kann.|I wonder who can help me.}}

====Usage notes====
* Generally, ''fragen'' has always been a weak verb.
* The strong forms of ''fragen'' are secondary and now obsolete in formal writing. They live on in dialects and colloquial varieties.

